{
  "headline": "Parti Québécois wins election in Quebec, Canada",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Parti Québécois (PQ) won the Quebec provincial election on October 5, 2026, gaining its first power in 14 years. Led by Paul St-Pierre Plamondon, the party added 56 seats to its roster and more than doubled its vote share compared to the 2022 election. Despite the victory, the PQ fell five seats short of a majority and secured approximately 28% of the popular vote, which polling analyst Philippe Fournier identified as the lowest percentage for any government in Canadian electoral history. This lack of a majority complicates the party's promise to hold a referendum on separating the French-speaking province from Canada after Donald Trump leaves office in 2028. On October 6, 2026, Plamondon announced he would prevent the lieutenant governor, who represents the British crown, from swearing in his new government."
    },
    {
      "text": "The election results reflect a province where the vast majority of voters chose parties that do not seek a referendum. According to reports from The Guardian World and CBC Canada, the PQ faces several challenges, including opposition to plans to reduce immigration levels from the business community and farmers, and initiatives to apply strict language laws to colleges and abolish the monarchy. While Plamondon pledged in his victory speech to be a “good-faith collaborator” with the rest of the country, the province's most popular politician remains Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney, largely due to his resistance to Donald Trump. The PQ's rise follows the fall of the incumbent Coalition Avenir Québec."
    }
  ]
}

