  "headline": "Oil pipeline damaged in sabotage attack near Tel Hamis, Syria",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "An oil pipeline near Tel Hamis village in Syria’s northeastern Hasakah Governorate was damaged in a sabotage attack on Wednesday, according to the Syrian Petroleum Company (SPC). Following the incident, the SPC stated that its maintenance and emergency teams traveled to the site, secured the area, and isolated the damaged section to begin repairs."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "President Mahama directs Ghana's Finance Minister to allocate GH¢1bn for National Housing Fund in 2027 budget",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama has directed the Minister of Finance to allocate GH¢1 billion in the 2027 Budget as the government’s contribution to a GH¢3 billion National Housing Fund (NHF). The announcement was made by President Mahama on October 7, 2026, during the opening of the maiden National Conference on Housing Finance in Accra, Ghana. The funds are intended to be used on a revolving basis to finance housing projects and expand access to affordable housing. According to the President, more than 1,000 people have been supported to own homes through interventions involving the Fund and partner financial institutions. Figures presented at the conference indicated that Ghana has a housing deficit of 1.8 million units."
    },
    {
      "text": "Addressing the housing challenge, President Mahama stated that the issue involves both the availability of houses and the ability of households to secure affordable financing. He noted that self-employed workers, such as traders, artisans, farmers, and transport operators, often face difficulties securing mortgages because they may lack the payslips or conventional banking records required by providers. Consequently, he urged financial institutions to develop alternative methods for assessing repayment capacity, such as using verifiable business cash flows and savings histories. Regarding current rates, the NHF’s Mortgage Scheme offers cedi-denominated mortgages at 8.4 per cent, a decrease from 13.5 per cent, while developer financing under the scheme is set at 10.4 per cent."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "EU negotiators travel to China to discuss curbing hybrid electric car imports",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "EU trade negotiators are traveling to China for two days of talks aimed at reaching a deal to curb the export of cheap hybrid electric cars into Europe. The negotiations, led by Trade Commissioner Maroš Šefčovič and his team, are scheduled to begin on Thursday and continue through October 2, 2026. Negotiators are reportedly seeking a “proof of concept” pilot agreement in the automotive sector that could potentially be scaled to other industries in future months. The effort seeks to address Beijing’s trade surplus, which has reached £1bn a day, and aims to achieve “tangible, meaningful and measurable” results ahead of an EU leaders' meeting in Brussels on October 1, 2026, where China is a high-priority agenda item. Sources indicate the mood in the talks has shifted as EU member states have increased demands for protection against Chinese imports that threaten domestic industries."
    },
    {
      "text": "Diplomatic efforts to prevent a full-scale trade war began in late June. Last month, the EU requested that China voluntarily restrict hybrid exports to address rising sales, warning that failure to do so could result in safeguards, such as quotas. Following the imposition of EU tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles in October 2024, there was an increase in the export of hybrid cars, which were not subject to those tariffs. While the EU has sought these restrictions, China's specific response to the request for voluntary limits is not known. Sources have noted historical difficulty in curbing Chinese exports to the EU due to the country's ability to pivot quickly when trade barriers are implemented."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh Bank allows foreign currency accounts for approved foreign loan proceeds",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Bangladesh Bank (BB) has authorized designated authorized dealer (AD) banks to open and maintain foreign currency (FC) accounts for eligible private sector industrial borrowers to hold proceeds from approved foreign loans. According to a report by BSS, the central bank issued this instruction on a Wednesday to facilitate the cross-border settlement of payments related to these loans. Under the circular, private sector industrial enterprises in Bangladesh may access medium- and long-term external financing provided they receive prior approval from competent authorities. Authorized dealer banks are required to obtain and retain copies of specific approvals, such as those from the Scrutiny Committee on Foreign Loan or Supplier's Credit of the Invest Bangladesh Authority, or from the Standing Committee on Non-Concessional Loan (SCNCL) and Bangladesh Bank where applicable."
    },
    {
      "text": "Balances held in these FC accounts may be used for import payments following prevailing Import Policy Orders and foreign exchange regulations, as well as for bona fide service payments necessary for project implementation. For service payments, banks must conduct due diligence, including the deduction and payment of applicable taxes and VAT, and obtain competent authority approval where required. The circular also permits the encashment of foreign currency into Taka from these accounts for local procurement, subject to relevant approval provisions. These accounts may earn interest or profit based on the terms of the banker-customer relationship. Authorized dealer banks must report the utilization of loan proceeds to the Foreign Exchange Investment Department (FEID) of Bangladesh Bank on a quarterly basis, covering the quarters ending in December, March, June, and September. Once the drawdown and utilization of the loan proceeds are complete, the accounts must be closed and the FEID informed, though accounts may be maintained until maturity if the loan agreement requires fund retention for repayment."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana's NPP celebrates Dr. Mahamudu Bawumia's birthday and expresses hope for 2028 election victory",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The New Patriotic Party (NPP) in Ghana has issued a message celebrating the birthday of Dr. Mahamudu Bawumia, the party's 2024 presidential candidate and former Vice President. In a statement released on behalf of the NPP's national leadership and membership, the party commended Dr. Bawumia for his years of service to the party and the Republic of Ghana, describing his public service journey as an inspiration to those within the party's political tradition and beyond. The NPP extended congratulations and offered prayers for Dr. Bawumia's health, strength, wisdom, courage, and divine favor regarding his future responsibilities. The party also expressed its hope that Dr. Bawumia would win the 2028 General Election to serve as President of the Republic of Ghana, stating, “May the good Lord guide your steps, bless the work of your hands, unite the Party behind our common purpose and, in accordance with His divine will, grant you victory in the 2028 General Election to serve as President of the Republic of Ghana.”"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "ECNEC approves 13 projects in Bangladesh totaling Tk 162 billion",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Executive Committee of the National Economic Council (ECNEC) approved 13 projects involving a total cost of Tk 162 billion on October 7, 2026. The approval occurred during the committee's fifth meeting at the Bangladesh Secretariat, chaired by Prime Minister and ECNEC Chairman Tarique Rahman. Of the 13 projects, 10 are new, two are revised, and one involves an extension of an implementation period. State Minister for Planning Zonayed Abdur Rahim Saki briefed reporters following the meeting. The total funding for the approved projects includes Tk 106 billion from government funds, Tk 40.8 billion from project loans, and Tk 15.2 billion from the funds of concerned agencies. One of the approved projects is a Tk 24.9 billion plan to construct a 442-megawatt peak (MWp) solar power plant at Rampal in Bagerhat."
    },
    {
      "text": "The Rampal solar power plant project, proposed by the Power Division and to be implemented by the Bangladesh Power Development Board (BPDB), aims to increase renewable energy generation, diversify primary fuel sources, and reduce carbon emissions. The Planning Commission recommended implementation from September 2026 to March 2029. The plant will be built on 918.50 acres of land in Block-B of Rampal, which the BPDB previously acquired in 2012 for a coal-fired power plant. The Planning Commission stated that using this existing land would eliminate the need for fresh land acquisition and reduce costs. The project will be financed by the BPDB using Tk 3.74 billion from its own funds and Tk 21.23 billion from the Power Sector Development Fund. The latter loan carries a 2 per cent interest rate with a one-year grace period and a 16-year repayment period. Once implemented, the plant is expected to add 442 MW of electricity to the national grid and utilize existing transmission infrastructure from the nearby Rampal coal-fired power plant. The Planning Commission noted the project would support the government's target of reaching a 20 per cent renewable energy share in the total energy mix by 2030. Currently, solar power generation in the country stands at 797 MW out of a total on-grid capacity of 28,827 MW."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "United Kingdom removes former Syrian Economy Minister from sanctions list",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The United Kingdom removed Houmam al-Jazairi, the former Syrian Minister of Economy and Foreign Trade, from its sanctions list on October 6, 2026, according to a notice from the UK Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office. Al-Jazairi, who served as minister from 2014 to 2016, had been under UK sanctions since October 2014. The previous sanctions designation identified him as subject to a travel ban, an asset freeze, and director disqualification. The UK government's notice stated that the designation was revoked under the Syria sanctions regime, though British authorities did not provide specific reasons for the decision."
    }
  ]
}
